WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.260
What's the ideal beginner material
when you start a new language?

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:08.430
In my view, it's essentially what
we have with the mini stories,

00:00:08.435 --> 00:00:09.570
and I'm gonna explain why.

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:14.520
I think there are certain elements
that you must have in an effective

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:17.220
beginner book or beginner material.

00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:22.920
Number one, you have to have a
lot of high frequency words, and

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:29.550
especially high frequency verbs, the
doings, the give, take, want, need.

00:00:30.150 --> 00:00:31.110
Absolutely essential.

00:00:32.070 --> 00:00:36.510
So high frequency verbs, but generally
speaking, high frequency words.

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:41.820
The second thing you need is you
need to have material where the same

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:45.330
words repeat often in each lesson.

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:46.530
Okay?

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:52.260
All too often in the kind of, uh,
beginner book that you buy, where

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:55.800
they take you from, uh, you know, the
airport to the train station, to the

00:00:55.830 --> 00:00:58.650
doctor's office, to uh, grocery store.

00:00:59.115 --> 00:01:02.504
You're basically dealing with
new vocabulary in every lesson.

00:01:03.825 --> 00:01:08.835
And the, the other thing that's wrong with
a lot of the other beginner material is

00:01:08.835 --> 00:01:16.065
that they have a lot of explanation, a
lot of, you know, quiz tests, drills, and

00:01:16.065 --> 00:01:19.215
very little sort of content to learn from.

00:01:20.445 --> 00:01:22.575
Uh, with our mini stories,
it's the opposite.

00:01:22.575 --> 00:01:28.005
It's entirely the story, but the
story repeats in a way five times.

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:33.960
Because you hear the same story spoken in
one person, like the first person or the

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:39.780
third person, or in one tense, and then
in another tense or in another person, and

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:44.220
then you have a statement, then you have
a question on that statement, and then you

00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:46.440
have either a positive or negative answer.

00:01:46.699 --> 00:01:53.955
So in all, you get five, more or less
five, uh, occurrences of more or less the

00:01:53.955 --> 00:01:56.625
same words in slightly different forms.

00:01:56.774 --> 00:01:58.304
That's very reinforcing.

00:01:58.845 --> 00:02:03.945
And the by the second or third time you
that you hear these words, you are now in

00:02:03.945 --> 00:02:07.365
a position you could even start imitating
them because they're starting to be,

00:02:07.370 --> 00:02:10.905
become sort of form part of your brain.

00:02:11.445 --> 00:02:15.825
So first of all, high frequency,
especially high frequency verbs.

00:02:16.155 --> 00:02:18.195
Second of all, lots of repetition.

00:02:18.704 --> 00:02:22.424
The third thing you need is
lots of content, lots of them.

00:02:22.695 --> 00:02:26.475
That's the other problem with traditional
language books is you get a very

00:02:26.475 --> 00:02:29.685
short snippet with a lot of exercises.

00:02:30.075 --> 00:02:36.195
In ideal, in an ideal sort of beginner
course or material like our mini stories,

00:02:36.435 --> 00:02:38.505
you wanna have predominantly content.

00:02:38.925 --> 00:02:43.785
Now, I'm assuming that the average
person who starts in a language will

00:02:43.785 --> 00:02:46.695
buy a book, uh, hopefully a small book.

00:02:47.325 --> 00:02:52.095
And I'm very much in favor of buying
the smallest grammar book you can find.

00:02:52.575 --> 00:02:58.424
And this, uh, particular series, uh,
Essential German Grammar, very thin.

00:02:58.995 --> 00:03:00.765
Italian Grammar, very thin.

00:03:01.424 --> 00:03:03.425
Uh, here's one that's Czech Grammar.

00:03:03.975 --> 00:03:06.505
It's a little thicker and
has a lot of examples.

00:03:06.945 --> 00:03:10.245
Examples are good if you're gonna go
for a book, and I think most people

00:03:10.245 --> 00:03:15.045
should buy a book as a companion to
the sort of ideal beginner material,

00:03:15.045 --> 00:03:16.575
beginner course that I'm talking about.

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:21.750
In our mini stories, we don't take you
to the train station and to the, uh,

00:03:21.750 --> 00:03:26.760
you know, customs and all of the sort
of traditional scenarios that most

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:33.480
beginner books use, because I don't
believe that you will learn sort of the

00:03:33.485 --> 00:03:39.690
script for a given scenario that's gonna
enable you then to function comfortably

00:03:40.350 --> 00:03:42.360
at the train station or at customs.

00:03:42.810 --> 00:03:45.640
You have to kind of elevate
your, your whole game.

00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:50.715
And therefore, again, the ideal
beginner material, which should have,

00:03:50.715 --> 00:03:53.295
you know, high frequency, uh, verbs.

00:03:53.505 --> 00:03:57.405
There should be a lot of it, like
we have 60 mini stories, but it

00:03:57.405 --> 00:03:58.995
should be about everyday life.

00:03:59.174 --> 00:04:02.864
Things that happen all the time,
things that are familiar to you, not

00:04:02.870 --> 00:04:07.695
with the idea that you will, you know,
master a script for a given situation.

00:04:07.695 --> 00:04:12.614
I think that's an unrealistic expectation,
but rather that you can get comfortable

00:04:12.885 --> 00:04:15.704
in a wide variety of situations.

00:04:16.005 --> 00:04:18.975
You'll hear the same structures
over and over again, you'll, you'll

00:04:19.065 --> 00:04:23.595
hear the same verbs over and over
again, and that then helps the brain

00:04:23.595 --> 00:04:24.975
to get used to this new language.

00:04:25.995 --> 00:04:31.725
Bear in mind that my strategy with our
many stories is I listen to them 20,

00:04:31.725 --> 00:04:36.705
30 times, not at one sitting, but I end
up listening to them 20 or 30 times.

00:04:36.705 --> 00:04:38.175
I know that from our statistics.

00:04:38.925 --> 00:04:43.215
Another thing about the mini stories,
and this is why I'm fond of the many

00:04:43.215 --> 00:04:48.030
stories, is after you've spent a fair
amount of time exploring the language

00:04:48.030 --> 00:04:52.200
with more interesting content, and
you're going to be in a situation where

00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:56.070
you're gonna have to use the language,
which is now my situation with Polish.

00:04:57.390 --> 00:05:01.350
I'm going back to the mini stories because
the mini stores are like going to the gym.

00:05:01.980 --> 00:05:07.440
And so I'm going back to the mini stories,
reviewing, refreshing, uh, picking

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:11.970
up on some of the, sort of call them
basic details that I once learned then

00:05:11.970 --> 00:05:14.460
forgot, you know, learned again, forgot.

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:18.210
Now I want to go back in
there and refresh my brain.

00:05:18.420 --> 00:05:20.520
Work out, have a workout in the language.

00:05:20.760 --> 00:05:24.000
And the mini stories are
excellent for that purpose.

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:29.490
So, you know, your ideal, uh,
beginner material should be material

00:05:29.730 --> 00:05:33.300
that you can go back to later on
as sort of a training exercise.

00:05:33.719 --> 00:05:38.040
That means that it's not like there's
a lesson one followed by two, and

00:05:38.040 --> 00:05:42.030
that you're gradually sort of getting
into more and more difficult material.

00:05:42.585 --> 00:05:44.415
And that you start with,
"hello, how are you?"

00:05:44.415 --> 00:05:46.635
"My name is basically".

00:05:46.635 --> 00:05:48.195
You're just put into the language.

00:05:48.375 --> 00:05:51.555
You're put into lesson than
one is a particular situation.

00:05:52.065 --> 00:05:55.515
Lesson two is some other situation,
and there's enough repetition that

00:05:55.515 --> 00:05:59.955
even starting from scratch, as I have
done on many occasions, I can cope.

00:06:00.165 --> 00:06:04.995
I gradually start to save words
and phrases, and I listen and I

00:06:04.995 --> 00:06:08.595
read, and slowly, slowly, slowly, I
become familiar with the language.

00:06:09.075 --> 00:06:14.265
But it's not at a level of like for
someone who knows nothing, it's actually

00:06:14.265 --> 00:06:19.195
at a, call it lower intermediate
level, eventually moving to a genuine,

00:06:19.425 --> 00:06:21.225
genuine sort of intermediate level.

00:06:21.405 --> 00:06:26.505
So when you go back to it, you're still
in sort of normal language, spoken at

00:06:26.505 --> 00:06:30.975
a normal speed so that the material,
the beginner material, now becomes an

00:06:30.975 --> 00:06:33.225
excellent, excellent training facility.

00:06:33.585 --> 00:06:38.609
I should also add that an ideal
beginner book in today's day and age

00:06:38.640 --> 00:06:42.030
should be available in digital form.

00:06:42.179 --> 00:06:44.849
So you can be on a computer or on an iPad.

00:06:45.150 --> 00:06:47.460
You can click on Words to Save them.

00:06:47.465 --> 00:06:48.840
You can click on them to hear them.

00:06:49.080 --> 00:06:51.090
You can hear the audio for that sentence.

00:06:51.510 --> 00:06:55.599
Uh, there's so many bits of
functionality like we have at LingQ,

00:06:55.599 --> 00:06:57.419
but not only at LingQ others as well.

00:06:57.869 --> 00:07:01.349
There's so much functionality
that's available if we're

00:07:01.349 --> 00:07:03.000
in a digital environment.

00:07:03.359 --> 00:07:06.239
And of course it's nice
to read a paper book.

00:07:06.645 --> 00:07:10.335
And of course it's, it's nice to have
as a companion, you know, a paper

00:07:10.335 --> 00:07:16.245
book, but primarily in order to get
the language in our brain, we need

00:07:16.245 --> 00:07:22.125
this kind of material that has audio
and text and ability to find meanings

00:07:22.130 --> 00:07:25.695
of words quickly and, and without
having a thumb through a dictionary.

00:07:25.965 --> 00:07:30.225
And, and of course if we add into that
the sort of repetition, high frequency

00:07:30.465 --> 00:07:36.390
and so forth, we have material that can
give us a, a, a foothold in the language.

00:07:36.630 --> 00:07:41.490
And from there we can then expand
following our interests, more

00:07:41.490 --> 00:07:44.909
compelling content and go as far
as we want to go in the language.

00:07:44.909 --> 00:07:49.110
But I think that beginner material
is so important to get us started.

00:07:49.770 --> 00:07:55.469
And, uh, in my view, the, you won't be
surprised that I say this because I was

00:07:55.469 --> 00:08:00.690
the one who basically, uh, specked out how
I wanted the mini stories to be written.

00:08:01.155 --> 00:08:03.854
I think that's the best way
to get started in a language.

00:08:04.005 --> 00:08:05.505
So thank you for listening.

00:08:05.835 --> 00:08:06.255
Bye for now.

